 ## Overview
-The Airlight Point Spread Function (PSF) models the angular distribution of light scattered by a participating medium, treating the medium as a blur kernel that modifies incident radiance. It solves the problem of efficiently approximating multiple scattering effects in real-time rendering by decoupling the geometric scattering profile from the medium's attenuation properties. Unlike direct numerical integration of the radiative transfer equation, this method relies on a normalized angular function that can be precomputed or factored, enabling interactive parameter changes without expensive recomputation.
+The Airlight Point Spread Function (PSF) models the angular distribution of scattered light in a participating medium, treating the medium as a blur kernel that modifies incident radiance [S1]. It approximates multiple scattering effects in real-time rendering by normalizing radiance into a function of the scattering angle $\gamma$, avoiding costly direct numerical simulation of the radiative transfer equation [S1]. This method builds on [PSF-based Multiple Scattering Rendering](1297-psf-based-multiple-scattering-rendering.md) and differs from [Narrow beam distribution method](9069-narrow-beam-distribution-method.md) and [PSF Modeling](21315-psf-modeling.md) by explicitly normalizing radiance by source intensity and distance to isolate the angular scattering profile [S1].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Inputs:** Source intensity $I_0$, source-to-viewer distance $D_{sv}$, viewer-to-point distance $D_{vp}$, scattering coefficient $\beta$, and scattering angle $\gamma$.
